---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'server' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Enforce: what's coming"
  description="Enforce is the direction, not a shipped product: an authoritative, platform-level merge check. See what's planned for GitHub and Azure DevOps, and why no single server architecture is promised yet.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b> / enforce</p>
          <h1>Enforce: what's coming</h1>
          <p class="lede measure"><strong>Assist</strong> and <strong>warn</strong> are here today, entirely through the extension. <strong>Enforce</strong> — an authoritative merge check that runs at the platform level, independently of any reviewer's browser — is the direction this is heading, not something you can install today. This page describes the intent, not a product.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Why nothing ships here yet</h2>
          <p>An authoritative check has to run somewhere other than a reviewer's browser, and the default plan is to use whatever is simplest and most native to each platform — not to stand up one general-purpose backend:</p>
          <ul class="bul">
            <li>GitHub would default to the simplest GitHub-native or lightweight mechanism available.</li>
            <li>Azure DevOps would default to the simplest Azure DevOps-native or lightweight mechanism available.</li>
            <li>A CCT backend isn't part of the plan by default. It would only enter the picture if a concrete requirement later shows that a platform-native integration genuinely can't do the job.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="small">Committing to one generic server architecture now, before any of this is validated against a real platform, would lock the project into a shape most platforms don't need. So this page names a destination, not a mechanism — and no server is assumed along the way.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>What already works without it</h2>
          <p>The extension doesn't wait for enforce to be useful. In its strict mode, it can already refuse to submit a malformed comment — a guardrail in the browser, not a merge gate on the platform. See <a href={href('howitworks', 'en')}>how it works</a> for the distinction between what the extension can stop today and what only a platform-level check will eventually be able to guarantee.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Want to help shape it?</h2>
          <p>If your team would need enforcement on a particular platform sooner rather than later, open an issue on the repository and describe your setup: which platform, self-hosted or not, and roughly how many repositories would be affected. That's the kind of information used to decide what gets built first.</p>
        </section>
        <div class="pager"></div>
</Docs>
